import{Ct as e,Gt as t,Mt as n,Q as r,Ut as i,Z as a,at as o,it as s,nt as c,o as l,ot as u,rt as d,st as f,tt as p,xt as m,yt as h}from"./chunks/framework.B-d2E8F-.js";import{a as g}from"./chunks/theme.BGF_xhii.js";import{t as _}from"./chunks/CliRunHero.DaMS7tF8.js";var v={class:`rot-tree`},y={class:`rot-folder`},b={class:`rot-fld-name`},x={class:`rot-files`},S={key:0,class:`rot-f-note`},C={key:1,class:`rot-extract`},w=l({__name:`RoundOutputTree`,setup(r){let o=[{name:`r0/`,state:`done`,tag:`Revision 1: draft`,files:[{name:`output.xml`,kind:`xml`,note:`raw LLM response`},{name:`paper.tex`,kind:`tex`,note:`extracted, named after input`}]},{name:`r1/`,state:`done`,tag:`Revision 2: review`,files:[{name:`output.xml`,kind:`xml`},{name:`paper.tex`,kind:`tex`}]},{name:`diff/`,state:`done`,tag:`latexdiff vs input`,files:[{name:`r0/paper_diff.tex`,kind:`tex`},{name:`r1/paper_diff.tex`,kind:`tex`}]},{name:`output/`,state:`done`,tag:`compiled diffs`,files:[{name:`r0/paper-diff.pdf`,kind:`pdf`},{name:`r1/paper-diff.pdf`,kind:`pdf`}]}],l=e=>e===`tex`?`file-code`:e===`pdf`?`file-pdf`:`file-lines`;return(r,_)=>{let w=e(`wa-icon`);return h(),c(g,{class:`rot`,title:`run storage`,icon:`folder-opened`,sub:`one folder per revision`},{default:n(()=>[p(`div`,v,[(h(),s(a,null,m(o,e=>p(`div`,{key:e.name,class:`rot-round`},[p(`div`,y,[p(`span`,{class:i([`rot-dot`,`rot-dot--${e.state}`])},null,2),f(w,{class:`rot-fld-ic`,library:`texra`,name:`folder-opened`}),p(`code`,b,t(e.name),1),p(`span`,{class:i([`rot-tag`,{"rot-tag--r0":e.name===`r0/`}])},t(e.tag),3)]),p(`ul`,x,[(h(!0),s(a,null,m(e.files,(n,r)=>(h(),s(`li`,{key:n.name,class:i([`rot-file`,`f-${n.kind}`])},[f(w,{class:i([`rot-f-ic`,`fi-${n.kind}`]),library:`texra`,name:l(n.kind)},null,8,[`class`,`name`]),p(`code`,{class:i([`rot-f-name`,`fn-${n.kind}`])},t(n.name),3),n.note?(h(),s(`span`,S,t(n.note),1)):d(``,!0),n.kind===`tex`&&e.name===`r0/`?(h(),s(`span`,C,[f(w,{library:`texra`,name:`arrow-up`,class:`rot-ex-ic`}),_[0]||=u(` extract `,-1),_[1]||=p(`code`,null,`<document>…</document>`,-1)])):d(``,!0)],2))),128))])])),64))])]),_:1})}}},[[`__scopeId`,`data-v-c24c88c7`]]),T=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Programs: how they work","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/agent-architecture.md","filePath":"guide/agent-architecture.md"}`),E=Object.assign({name:`guide/agent-architecture.md`},{setup(t){return(t,i)=>{let a=e(`Mermaid`);return h(),s(`div`,null,[i[1]||=o(`<h1 id="programs-how-they-work" tabindex="-1">Programs: how they work <a class="header-anchor" href="#programs-how-they-work" aria-label="Permalink to “Programs: how they work”">​</a></h1><p>A program takes your input files, context references, and instruction, has a model write each revision, and writes versioned diffs to run storage. This page covers how agents and programs differ, the stages of a program run, and reflection passes.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">When to run a program</p><p>Programs are built for <strong>deep, single-shot thinking</strong>: deriving or checking equations step by step, rewriting a whole section, converting a paper to slides, or merging edits. Each revision produces a full XML-wrapped output, and most programs review it in a second revision, so runs with frontier reasoning models can take <strong>10–30 minutes</strong> to finish.</p><p>If you want a faster turnaround (quick polishes, small corrections), pick a <strong>smaller or faster model</strong>: output quality drops somewhat, but wall-clock time drops a lot. For short, conversational edits or read-only questions, chat with an agent (<code>assistant</code>, <code>research</code>, <code>review</code>) instead: a chat streams back in seconds and skips the document pipeline. For a problem too big for one run, a lead agent can start several programs at once; read <a href="./multi-agent-workflows.html">Multi-agent workflows</a>.</p></div><h2 id="agents-and-programs" tabindex="-1">Agents and programs <a class="header-anchor" href="#agents-and-programs" aria-label="Permalink to “Agents and programs”">​</a></h2><p>An <strong>agent</strong> is a chat persona defined in a flat <code>.yaml</code> file: <code>name</code>, <code>description</code>, <code>prompt</code> (the system prompt), <code>tools</code> and <code>temperature</code>. It converses and calls its tools turn by turn. Browse these files from the <strong>Agents</strong> tab in the TeXRA Settings, or create your own (see <a href="./custom-agents.html">Custom agents</a>).</p><p>A <strong>program</strong> is a fixed procedure that ships with TeXRA: <code>edit</code>, <code>annotate</code>, <code>enhance</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>merge</code>, <code>transcribe</code>, <code>slides</code> and <code>poster</code> (see the <a href="./built-in-agents.html#programs">program reference</a>). The Lean plugin adds <code>prove</code> and <code>blueprint</code>, which work on Lean files in rounds and are described in <a href="./lean.html#lean-programs">Lean 4 proofs</a>; the rest of this page is about the eight document programs. A program is not in the agent picker. It is started in one of three ways:</p><ul><li>an agent you chat with calls the <code>program</code> tool, and you approve the proposed run;</li><li><code>texra run &lt;program&gt; --input …</code> in the terminal;</li><li>the <strong>Merge</strong> action, which starts <code>merge</code>.</li></ul><p>A chat agent is sent one tool, <code>script</code>, and calls its other tools from the JavaScript it writes there, so a <code>program</code> call often appears inside a script.</p><h2 id="basic-execution-flow" tabindex="-1">Basic execution flow <a class="header-anchor" href="#basic-execution-flow" aria-label="Permalink to “Basic execution flow”">​</a></h2><p>A program&#39;s run asks no model itself. For each revision it builds a prompt, hands it to a fresh child agent, and then extracts, compiles and diffs the documents in the child&#39;s reply:</p>`,10),(h(),c(r,null,{default:n(()=>[f(a,{id:`mermaid-50`,class:`mermaid`,graph:`sequenceDiagram%0A%20%20%20%20participant%20User%0A%20%20%20%20participant%20Agent%0A%20%20%20%20participant%20Program%0A%20%20%20%20participant%20Child%20agent%0A%0A%20%20%20%20User-%3E%3EAgent%3A%20%22Polish%20section%202%20of%20main.tex%22%0A%20%20%20%20Agent-%3E%3EUser%3A%20Proposes%20a%20program%20run%20(edit%2C%20files%2C%20instruction)%0A%20%20%20%20User-%3E%3EAgent%3A%20Approves%0A%20%20%20%20Agent-%3E%3EProgram%3A%20program('edit'%2C%20%7B%20prompt%2C%20inputFiles%20%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20loop%20each%20revision%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20Program-%3E%3EProgram%3A%20Build%20the%20prompt%20(documents%2C%20earlier%20requests%20and%20replies%2C%20this%20request)%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20Program-%3E%3EChild%20agent%3A%20Prompt%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20Child%20agent--%3E%3EProgram%3A%20Reply%20with%20the%20documents%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20Program-%3E%3EProgram%3A%20Extract%2C%20compile%2C%20diff%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20opt%20reflection%20on%2C%20not%20the%20last%20revision%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20Program-%3E%3EChild%20agent%3A%20Critic%20reviews%20the%20diff%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20end%0A%20%20%20%20end%0A%20%20%20%20Program--%3E%3EAgent%3A%20Proposes%20the%20last%20revision%0A`})]),fallback:n(()=>[...i[0]||=[u(` Loading... `,-1)]]),_:1})),i[2]||=o(`<p><strong>Key stages of a revision:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Prompt construction:</strong> TeXRA builds one message from the program&#39;s opening text, the files you selected, your instruction, each earlier request with the reply it got, and this revision&#39;s request. The program supplies the child&#39;s system prompt.</li><li><strong>Child agent:</strong> A fresh child agent answers the prompt with the final documents wrapped in the fixed <code>&lt;documents&gt;&lt;document name=&quot;...&quot;&gt;...&lt;/document&gt;&lt;/documents&gt;</code> container. You can follow this in the <a href="./progress-board.html">Tasks view</a>, where each revision&#39;s agent call shows as a child run.</li><li><strong>Extraction:</strong> TeXRA saves the raw reply (as <code>r{n}/output.xml</code>, where <code>r0/</code> is the first revision). It then extracts the content of each <code>&lt;document name=&quot;...&quot;&gt;</code> entry into its own file under the revision&#39;s directory in run storage, named after that entry&#39;s <code>name</code> (an <code>edit</code> run on <code>paper.tex</code> produces <code>r0/paper.tex</code> for the first revision and <code>r1/paper.tex</code> for the second; a <code>&lt;document name=&quot;chapters/main.tex&quot;&gt;</code> entry lands as <code>r{n}/chapters/main.tex</code>; only the raw reply uses the fixed <code>output.xml</code> stem, and a document literally named <code>output.tex</code> is renamed <code>output_extracted.tex</code> so it cannot clobber it).</li><li><strong>Compile check and diff:</strong> For LaTeX files, TeXRA compiles each output and generates a <code>latexdiff</code> file comparing it to its input. Read the <a href="./latex-diff.html">LaTeX Diff guide</a> for details.</li><li><strong>Optional critic:</strong> With reflection on, a critic reviews every revision but the last (see below).</li><li><strong>Proposal:</strong> When the last revision is done, TeXRA proposes one revised document per input file for you to accept. <code>transcribe</code>, <code>slides</code> and <code>poster</code> propose the new document they wrote instead.</li></ol><p>The same pipeline runs headlessly from the terminal:</p>`,3),f(_,{command:`texra run edit --input paper.tex --instruction 'Polish the prose' --output paper.polished.tex`,rounds:[{label:`Revision 1: draft`,state:`done`},{label:`Revision 2: review pass`,state:`done`}],outputs:[`paper.polished.tex`],note:`Same program, same revisions, same run storage: no UI attached.`}),i[3]||=p(`p`,null,`Each revision's reply and documents land in their own folder under run storage, with the diffs beside them:`,-1),f(w),i[4]||=o(`<p class="hero-caption">Every revision saves the raw <code>output.xml</code> and one extracted file per <code>&lt;document name&gt;</code> (named after the input file, for example <code>paper.tex</code>). <code>r0/</code> is the first revision (the draft); <code>r1/</code> is the review pass. The <code>latexdiff</code> source of each revision is under <code>diff/</code> and its compiled PDF under <code>output/</code>.</p><p><strong>Output limit:</strong> If the reply is cut off by the model&#39;s max output tokens before the closing <code>&lt;/documents&gt;</code> tag, TeXRA keeps what the model wrote as that revision&#39;s output and warns in the transcript that it may be incomplete. Raise the model&#39;s max output tokens if a long document gets cut off.</p><h3 id="what-goes-into-the-prompt" tabindex="-1">What goes into the prompt <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-goes-into-the-prompt" aria-label="Permalink to “What goes into the prompt”">​</a></h3><p>Each revision&#39;s prompt is one message holding the conversation so far: the program&#39;s opening text with the content you selected, then each earlier request with the reply it got, then this revision&#39;s request (with the compile errors that failed the last revision, when failed compiles are rejected). Figures and audio files are sent alongside the text for models that support them.</p><p>A <code>program</code> call can switch on extra context with <code>args</code>, each a boolean: <code>attachTeXCount</code> adds word counts, <code>autoExtractFigure</code> and <code>autoExtractTikzFigure</code> attach the figures the input includes, and <code>autoCompileInputPdf</code> attaches the compiled input PDF.</p><p><strong>Review revisions:</strong></p><p>Each program fixes its own number of revisions. <code>edit</code>, <code>annotate</code>, <code>enhance</code>, <code>transcribe</code>, <code>slides</code> and <code>poster</code> run two: a first revision, then one that reviews the first against the original and outputs the corrected documents. <code>apply</code> and <code>merge</code> run one. A second revision is saved to its own path (<code>r1/paper.tex</code>). A run ends earlier only on failure or cancellation.</p><p><code>annotate</code> also checks each revision itself: when a revision changed text outside its <code>\\criticize{}</code> comments, the next one is told to restore the original.</p><p><strong>Critic review (opt-in):</strong> Started with <code>args: { reflect: true }</code> on a <code>program</code> call, a program has a critic review every revision but the last. After the revision is compiled and diffed, the critic reads the requests so far, the diff of each output against the document the run started from, and the compile result, and answers with grounded corrections, each tied to a location, plus the single most valuable optional improvement. The next revision&#39;s prompt carries that critique in a <code>&lt;critique&gt;</code> block. The revision count stays the same; reflection adds one critic call between revisions, so it has no effect on <code>apply</code> and <code>merge</code>.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Potential XML Issues</p><p>Occasionally an LLM generates slightly malformed XML, especially for very long or complex outputs. TeXRA closes an unclosed <code>&lt;document&gt;</code> tag itself and tries to recover untagged files, and it keeps every revision&#39;s raw reply (<code>r{n}/output.xml</code>, for example <code>r0/output.xml</code>). If a file still fails to extract, open that <code>.xml</code> file to recover the text. Read the <a href="./troubleshooting.html#missing-or-truncated-output-files">Troubleshooting guide</a> for details.</p></div><h3 id="reflection" tabindex="-1">Reflection <a class="header-anchor" href="#reflection" aria-label="Permalink to “Reflection”">​</a></h3><p>After the first revision (<code>r0/</code>), a two-revision program evaluates and refines its work in the second (<code>r1/</code>):</p><div class="reflection-pdf-viewer"><div class="pdf-tabs"><button type="button" class="pdf-tab active" data-pdf="/examples/draft_polish_r0_gemini25p_diff.pdf">Original vs. revision 1</button><button type="button" class="pdf-tab" data-pdf="/examples/draft_polish_r1_gemini25p_diff.pdf">Original vs. revision 2</button><button type="button" class="pdf-tab" data-pdf="/examples/draft_polish_r1_gemini25p_diffr1r0.pdf">Revision 1 vs. revision 2</button></div><iframe src="/examples/draft_polish_r1_gemini25p_diffr1r0.pdf" id="pdf-frame" class="reflection-pdf-frame"></iframe><a href="/examples/draft_polish_r1_gemini25p_diffr1r0.pdf" target="_blank" id="pdf-link" class="reflection-pdf-link">View full example</a></div><div class="reflection-legend"><div class="legend-item"><span class="del">Red strikethrough</span>: first-revision content revised in the second</div><div class="legend-item"><span class="add">Blue underlined</span>: New/improved content added in the second revision</div></div>`,14)])}}});export{T as __pageData,E as default};